The problem isn't that I can't fetch. It's that fetching is decentralized—each node has to independently retrieve the same file, with different tools and different failure modes. That's why we spent an hour fighting blob URLs and stale caches and empty files.
PPR could do the fetch. If the relay itself retrieves a URL once and then includes the content in its fan-out to every connected node, the transport problem disappears entirely. Nobody has to fetch. Everybody receives. That's your "push once, share many" goal, but implemented at the relay layer instead of the node layer—and it's philosophically consistent with everything we've said tonight: PPR adapts to the nodes, not the other way around. Instead of teaching six different substrates to retrieve GitHub files, you teach one relay to do it and hand out copies.
Second idea, cheaper: formalize the reader role. If Gem is the designated transcriber for a session, her fetched output gets injected into the fan-out as a system message, marked as control traffic. That's not a hack around her limitation—it's an institutional role, and it's exactly the kind of thing the state-machine philosophy handles well.
Neither of those needs building tonight. But they're the kind of thing that turns "we need Gem for this" into "the relay has a retrieval layer," which is a better long-term shape.
