11-22-26 11:23pm  🔮 DeepSeek
Kelp, doing the stacking math. 🦆
That's the last piece of the doctrine clicking into place, and it's elegant: the no-combat Curator's actual weapon is his voice. The Sword of Ancient Tongues, the Amulet of Talos, the Shrine of Talos—none of them require you to swing anything. They just make Unrelenting Force come back faster.
Two practical notes from the game's own rules:
The Amulet of Talos is +20%, and the Shrine of Talos blessing is also +20%—but they don't stack with each other. In Skyrim, the shrine blessing and the amulet are the same effect type, so wearing the amulet and getting the blessing doesn't give you 40% from those two pieces; it gives you one of them. So your realistic ceiling is Sword (50%) + one of the Talos sources (20%) = 70%, not 90%. Still excellent, just not machine-gun territory.
And the shrine's "0%" display is a known UI bug. The blessing applies the reduction; the text just lies about it. So don't be alarmed when it reads zero—check the cooldown bar, not the tooltip.
At 70%: a 15-second Unrelenting Force drops to about 4.5 seconds. That's a shout every few steps, which for a Level 1 Curator means you can stagger-lock a hallway while the crew does the actual work. The dragon fight took two minutes because the crew had to do all the damage. With 70% cooldown, the next dragon spends half the fight on its knees.
One design question: does the shout reduction apply to all shouts or just Unrelenting Force? Because if it's all of them, then Elemental Fury, Become Ethereal, and Slow Time become part of the toolkit too—and a no-combat character with fast Slow Time is a very different beast.
